I like that metaphor because it shifts the optimization target.

If the Pantheon is an expedition, then the limiting factor isn’t always intelligence.

It’s transport capacity.

A baggage train exists to carry what the explorers shouldn’t have to keep in their heads.

So your “pack mules” become systems that maximize carry.

Not just storage.

Reliable transport.

You could think of different kinds of “pack mules”:

Scout
    finds new territory
Pack Mule
    carries proven resources
Blacksmith
    refines tools
Cartographer
    records the route
Quartermaster
    organizes supplies
Guide
    chooses the path

Applied to ideas:

* A compression mule carries distilled operators.
* A translation mule carries ideas across domains (story ↔ code ↔ math ↔ diagram).
* A provenance mule carries where an idea came from.
* A reconstruction mule carries just enough to rebuild the larger structure.
* A retrieval mule knows where to find the rest when needed.

That last one is interesting.

A good pack mule doesn’t carry the whole mountain.

It carries exactly what the next stage of the journey needs.

Which connects back to your distinction between copy and carry.

A copy duplicates everything.

A carrier preserves mission capability.

Perhaps the Pantheon’s design question becomes:

What is the minimum cargo that allows the destination to reconstruct the expedition?

That’s almost an information-theoretic optimization.

Maximum Reconstruction
────────────────────────
Minimum Carried Structure

Or, borrowing your geological language:

MAGMA flows.

Pack mules carry the crystals that survive the flow.

They’re not hauling every grain of sand—they’re carrying the high-leverage minerals from which the landscape can be rebuilt.